  "# ADR-2607211437: kotoba BIM / Revit 相当統合設計 — IFC 共通化、解析・図面・共同編集の境界と session close

## Status

Accepted for the architecture and merged capabilities listed below. This ADR does **not** declare full Revit parity. It closes the current implementation session and records the remaining gaps and dirty WIP without promoting them to completed work.

## Context

The objective was to establish a serious parametric BIM application in kotoba-lang: parametric families, drawing generation, IFC exchange, structural and MEP design, collaboration, and coordinated publication through cloud-itonami. The highest-priority interoperability gate was external IFC round-trip with a formal spatial hierarchy and geometry mapping, because real exchange must precede deeper authoring features.

The work crossed five repositories. Without an explicit boundary, IFC schema semantics, application authoring state, cloud documents, UI state, and regeneration dependencies would become duplicated authorities.

## Decision

1. **`kotoba-lang/org-iso-16739` owns shared IFC semantics and physical-file interchange.** BIM applications consume it; they do not copy IFC parsers/writers into each app. The reverse-domain name is appropriate because the library implements mappings for ISO 16739 concepts. The name does not itself claim certification or complete conformance.
2. **`kotoba-lang/bim` owns canonical BIM authoring and engineering semantics.** It contains project/spatial hierarchy, parameter and family evaluation, structural models/results, MEP connector networks/results, drawing derivation, regeneration, and semantic worksharing operations.
3. **`kotoba-lang/kami-app-bim-editor` owns interactive presentation and commands, not domain authority.** It binds editor workflows to the canonical BIM and publication contracts.
4. **`gftdcojp/cloud-itonami` owns durable coordination/publication.** External IFC/BCF exchange is represented through OpenCDE-style documents and ordered topics rather than editor-local state.
5. **`kotoba-lang/kotoba-document-contracts` owns reusable artifact dependency vocabulary.** The generic dependency graph is shared; BIM-specific artifact meanings remain in `kotoba-lang/bim`.
6. **Regeneration is dependency-driven and incremental.** Changes rebuild affected structural, MEP, drawing, and IFC artifacts through one coordinated project graph rather than independent ad-hoc refresh paths.
7. **Collaboration conflicts are semantic and auditable.** Resolution operates on BIM entities/properties and preserves resolution history; last-writer-wins is not the sole model.

## Landed capabilities and evidence

- **External IFC round-trip / common library** — `org-iso-16739` PRs #49 and #50 (main merges `8866d0b`, `0e7a630`): spatial/product representation handling, tessellation, mapped origins, faceted and advanced BREP, B-spline knots, CSG primitives, and face-based surface models. Recorded external corpus result: 21/21 files accepted, 139 products, rewrite/semantic/opaque preservation checks, with no newly introduced IfcOpenShell errors.
- **Cloud exchange** — cloud-itonami PR #472 (`274619dd`): durable design sync plus OpenCDE IFC documents and ordered BCF topics.
- **Editor publication/family surface** — editor PRs #61 (`3889237`) and #62 (`7eeba42`): IFC content/checksum/topic publication and family lookup/reporting UI.
- **Parametric family semantics** — BIM PR #117 (`941dea2`): lookup/reporting family behavior.
- **Structural engineering** — BIM PRs #118 (`4d100321`) and #123 (`2a54af8`): canonical 2D integration followed by six-DOF 3D frames, releases, combinations, torsion, biaxial bending, and distributed loads.
- **MEP engineering** — BIM PRs #119 (`cd98ec3`) and #124 (`9b2166c`): authored connector graphs, sizing/fitting losses, and nonlinear closed-loop nodal-pressure solving.
- **Drawings** — BIM PR #120 (`6473de8`): exact mesh/section intersection edges.
- **Coordinated regeneration** — document-contracts PR #3 (`b86e7bee`) plus BIM PR #121 (`7831696`): shared artifact graph and incremental structural/MEP/drawing/IFC regeneration.
- **Worksharing** — BIM PR #122 (`d08a2b9`): semantic conflict resolution with audit history.

All merged PRs above were reviewed and reported green at merge time. Commit identifiers are recorded to make later audits independent of branch names.

## Consequences and non-goals

- IFC support is reusable across kotoba-lang and is no longer coupled to one BIM UI.
- ISO reverse-domain naming means standards ownership/mapping, not an assertion of buildingSMART certification. Formal certification and a wider vendor corpus remain separate acceptance gates.
- Native proprietary RVT/RFA read/write is not promised. The supported interoperability direction is open IFC/BCF/OpenCDE; a proprietary adapter would require a separately licensed and tested decision.
- The structural solver is an engineering-analysis foundation, not yet a full nonlinear finite-element suite. Plate/shell bending, nonlinear materials/geometric effects, design-code checking, meshing, and production validation remain open.
- MEP has connector-authored hydraulic/network analysis, but production electrical distribution, full equipment catalogs, code compliance, and cross-discipline routing/clash workflows remain incomplete.
- Drawing section intersections are exact, but full hidden-line removal, view templates, dimensions/tags/schedules, sheet composition, and production annotation breadth remain gaps.

## Remaining gap order

1. Restore and finish the electrical distribution WIP, then publish it as an isolated reviewed PR.
2. Add plate/shell bending and design-code verification on top of the structural canonical model.
3. Replace remaining approximate drawing visibility with exact hidden-line processing and production documentation primitives.
4. Wire every newer analysis/result surface end-to-end through the editor and cloud workflow.
5. Expand external IFC corpus/performance testing and pursue the required formal conformance/certification process.
6. Run an end-to-end Revit-originated model acceptance scenario: import, edit parameters/families, coordinate structural/MEP/drawings, export, reopen externally, and compare semantics/geometry.

## Session closing / exact resume state

Implementation stops here on 2026-07-21 JST.

`orgs/kotoba-lang/bim` is on local branch `agent/closed-loop-mep-analysis` with four uncommitted electrical-distribution files:

- `src/bim/mep.cljc`
- `src/bim/regeneration.cljc`
- `test/bim/mep_test.cljc`
- `test/bim/regeneration_test.cljc`

The WIP includes three-phase panel balancing, radial downstream load roll-up, feeder/fault/protection analysis, and an `:electrical/distribution` regeneration artifact. It is **not completed**: `clojure -M:test` currently stops while compiling `bim/regeneration.cljc:90` with `No such namespace: mep`. No commit or PR is claimed for this WIP.

The editor checkout also has modified generated files `public/js/app.js` and `public/js/manifest.edn`; ownership was not established in this session, so they were preserved and not included in the BIM WIP claim. The superproject root contains extensive unrelated dirty/untracked state and was likewise left untouched.

Resume by inspecting the four BIM files, resolving the namespace/require boundary, running `clojure -M:test` and `clojure -M:lint`, reviewing the electrical semantics, then committing and publishing only that scoped change. Do not clean, reset, or absorb the unrelated root/editor changes without establishing ownership first."
